d trace-class index datum** consists of a complex Hilbert space $\\mathcal H=\\mathcal H^+\\oplus\\mathcal H^-$, its grading operator $\\Gamma$ equal to $+I$ on $\\mathcal H^+$ and $-I$ on $\\mathcal H^-$, and a densely defined self-adjoint operator $Q$ such that $D(Q)$ is invariant under $\\Gamma$ and $Q\\Gamma\\psi=-\\Gamma Q\\psi$ for every $\\psi\\in D(Q)$. Set $H=Q^2$ on $D(H)=\\{\\psi\\in D(Q):Q\\psi\\in D(Q)\\}$ and require this composition operator to be nonnegative and self-adjoint. Assume $H$ has the orthogonal pure-point decomposition\n\n$$\\mathcal H=\\mathcal H_0^+\\oplus\\mathcal H_0^-\\oplus\\widehat{\\bigoplus}_{\\lambda\\in\\Sigma}(\\mathcal H_\\lambda^+\\oplus\\mathcal H_\\lambda^-),$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma\\subset(0,\\infty)$ is a supplied countable set of distinct eigenvalues, $\\mathcal H_0^\\pm=\\ker H\\cap\\mathcal H^\\pm$ are finite-dimensional, and $\\mathcal H_\\lambda^\\pm=\\ker(H-\\lambda I)\\cap\\mathcal H^\\pm$ are finite-dimensional eigenspaces contained in $D(H)$. Supply orthonormal bases of these subspaces whose union $E=E^+\\sqcup E^-$ is a Hilbert basis of $\\mathcal H$ compatible with energy and parity. Assume Countable Choice as used by the imported Hilbert-space, adjoint and trace-class definitions. The local positive-energy-pairing lemma derives, rather than assumes, the unitary map $Q/\\sqrt\\lambda:\\mathcal H_\\lambda^+\\to\\mathcal H_\\lambda^-$ and equality of their finite dimensions.\n\nFor each real $\\beta>0$, a bounded diagonal operator $T_\\beta$ is supplied as part of the data, with $T_\\beta e=e^{-\\beta\\lambda}e$ on $E_\\lambda^+\\cup E_\\lambda^-$ and $T_\\beta e=e$ on $E_0^+\\cup E_0^-$. Require $T_\\beta$ to be trace class for every $\\beta>0$; it is denoted $e^{-\\beta H}$. Its graded heat supertrace, called the **Witten index at parameter $\\beta$** in this data class, is\n\n$$W(\\beta):=\\sum_{e\\in E^+}\\langle T_\\beta e,e\\rangle-\\sum_{e\\in E^-}\\langle T_\\beta e,e\\rangle,$$\n\nwhere each family sum is in the finite-subset sense for absolutely summable scalar families. The supplied trace-class Hilbert-basis definition makes these sums absolutely convergent. Here $\\beta$ is only a positive real mathematical parameter and has no assigned physical units or temperature interpretation. The index datum is discrete: it contains no continuous spectral subspace or continuum regularization prescription, and makes no physical model identification.","provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Stephen P. Martin, A Supersymmetry Primer, version 7","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7","locator":"§7.1, printed pp. 68–70, Witten index and zero-energy states; this item adds exact pure-point trace-class data."},{"title":"S. J. Gates Jr., M. T. Grisaru, M. Roček and W. Siegel, Superspace, or One Thousand and One Lessons in Supersymmetry","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0108200","locator":"§8.4, supertrace context; finite multiplicities, domains and trace-class hypotheses are stated locally."}]},"proof_strategy":"not-applicable"}

