          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\mathcal H=\\mathcal H^+\\oplus\\mathcal H^-$ be a complex Hilbert space with grading operator $\\Gamma=+I$ on $\\mathcal H^+$ and $\\Gamma=-I$ on $\\mathcal H^-$. Let $Q$ be a densely defined self-adjoint operator with domain invariant under $\\Gamma$ and $Q\\Gamma\\psi=-\\Gamma Q\\psi$ for every $\\psi\\in D(Q)$. Set $H=Q^2$ on the composition domain $D(H)=\\{\\psi\\in D(Q):Q\\psi\\in D(Q)\\}$. For each $\\lambda>0$, let $E_\\lambda=\\{\\psi\\in D(H):H\\psi=\\lambda\\psi\\}$ be finite-dimensional, and put $E_\\lambda^\\pm=E_\\lambda\\cap\\mathcal H^\\pm$. Then $Q$ maps $E_\\lambda$ into itself, and\n\n$$U_\\lambda:=(Q/\\sqrt{\\lambda})|_{E_\\lambda^+}:E_\\lambda^+\\longrightarrow E_\\lambda^-$$\n\nis a unitary isomorphism, with inverse $(Q/\\sqrt{\\lambda})|_{E_\\lambda^-}$. In particular $\\dim E_\\lambda^+=\\dim E_\\lambda^-$. Also $Q\\psi=0$ for every zero-energy vector $\\psi\\in\\ker H$. No discrete spectrum or finite-multiplicity assumption is made about energies other than the selected $\\lambda$.",
